I'm in the middle of an extinctionist Tourist, and I decided to retry
Slash'em for a bit.  I had previously played some Slash'em, but the
best I'd ever gotten was being zapped for ~50hp by picking up both
artifacts on the chaotic quest.  None of my other characters passed
Sokoban/the Mines.

I decided to play a Lawful Valkyrie.  If overwhelming brute force isn't
enough then it's not overwhelming brute force.

The beginning game was rather similar to a vanilla game.  More varied
types of monsters, and I wasn't always sure what I could eat, and I
stayed away from anything too exotic.  Having a spear instead of a
sword slows you down a bit, but not that much (I did #enhance spear
though.  Better to lose a slot than to die from not giving out enough
damage).  Found studded leather, then scale mail.  Got to the mall, and
pet-id speed boots.  I also bought a magic candle for 20 zorkmids
(that's what the tripe ration cost).  No magic whistle or long sword,
though.  At some point here I also found a stethoscope lying around.
For most of the game I had a pet dog or cat for shops and eating troll
corpses.  I kept on losing them to various accidents though...

Armed with a permanent light source I did the first few mine levels
before sokoban.  I went around looking for a dwarf with a low armor
class and eventually (might be after mine town) found dwarven mithril
+2 which I wore for quite a while.  Mine town had a co-aligned altar,
and while I had found a longsword by this time, I sacced until I got
Mjollnir.  Not before getting two Monadic Devas first though.  I hadn't
ever gotten a pet minion before, so I wasn't sure what to do with them
(except for the obvious).  They didn't attack the priest or any of the
shopkeepers, so that was good.  They also don't seen to pick up
anything.  They should really be called moRonic devas, since they kept
on getting stuck in nooks in the dungeon.  I finally dipped a tin
whistle into a potion of gain level to be able to manage them.  After
that they were more helpful.  I fell through a trap door to the (real)
bottom of the mines, but didn't have any trouble there.

Getting back to minetown, I used the three diamonds I found to rack up
a credit line and get everything identified - one of the bigger
problems for a starting Valk in Vanilla.  Didn't really find anything
special.  The opera cloak I got off the vampire at the bottom of the
mines turned out to be magic resistance, so that was one worry down.
The minions also got rid of all the guards.  Also donated a bit to get
my AC down.  At this point I also found +1 leather gloves, and a +2
helmet (off minetown guards).  This was my armor until my ascension
run, though I did improve things a bit along the way (more protection,
a Hawaiian shirt, and some scrolls of enchant armor for my CoMR and
BoS).


Back at the mall I set up another credit line, and used that level as
my main base (though it was quite early in the dungeon, and made
stashing items a little tedious).  Did sokoban by squeezing past a few
boulders, and got a bag of holding.  I believe it to be a sign of my
growing skill that I prefer the "oR to the bag...  Anyway.

Next on the list was doing the quest.  By this point I had already been
hit by both a wand of cancellation (though it didn't seem to do
anything except to "shine sparks in my eyes" or something), and a wand
of draining (nasty!!), so I permanently left behind my small shield and
switched to #two-weaponing Excalibur and Mjollnir (which was my plan
all along).  IMHO this is one of the power-gaming issues that slashem
needs to fix, but I've got to admit that its really fun.  I'm sure
there are better weapon combinations than what I choose, given that
Slashem has so many artifacts (apparently even an artifact spoon!), but
even with both of them at +0 and 18 strength, I was already doing
around 50 points of damage every turn, which is good enough to kill
pretty much anything at this point in one or two turns.  The draining
resistance and searching that Ex gives are of course a bonus, and the
fact that both are practically guaranteed is the reason I chose a
lawful Valk in the first place.

By the time I got to the quest, I found a tinning kit, though it seems
that intrinsics are harder to get, since I only got heat resistance
after the third tin, and only got strength bonuses about 25% of the
time.  Lord Sutur wasn't too bad  (as usual).  I was more worried about
falling into the lava.

Got crowned around here.

I did the lawful and neutral quests next, though I kept going back to
my stash to store loot and every once in a while improve my equipment
(donated money once or twice).  The lawful quest was fun (ring of
increase accuracy -9 name polymorph control, bugswatter, etc).  I found
a black dragon corpse which I tinned with one of my few remaining
charges (eventually I actually read a scroll of charging for the
tinning kit - a first for me, but worth it).  This luckily made me feel
firm, which was good because I ended up practically playing
reflectionless the whole game though I did carry the Nighthorn in my
inventory (less of a chance of an artifact being cursed).  I did wield
Nighthorn for Medusa, but other than that, I just stood and took it.  I
believe that the corpses in the quest are there on purpose (to tease
you), so it's probably a good idea to have a tinning kit or a scroll of
charging ready when entering the lawful quest (there's was tinning kit
right there, but it's empty - hah,hah).

For the chaotic quest I enchanted both Excalibur and Mjollnir to +4
(bad experience with Vecna last time).  Other than the fact that I had
to hit him quite a few times, (does the half damage work on him?),
things weren't too bad.  I also got some of my stuff cursed.  This time
I was very careful picking up the chaotic artifacts....

One of the quests had an amulet of flying which I started wearing and
never took off.  It is incredibly convenient.  No more worrying about
pits, water, lava, or falling downstairs while burdened (maybe not even
while wielding rubber chickens, but I didn't risk it).  One problem it
-did cause was that I displaced no less than four pets into the water
(It drowns.  You feel guilty.).

Kept on heading down, every once in a while making a stash.  Did all
the sub levels without anything important happening.  Used the
Valk-smash for everything, even though I supposedly had some daggers
"in case".  I also kept a wands of fire/teleport/death/extra healing
(and ring of conflict in bag) in my main inventory in case of
emergencies, but never had any real reason to use them.  Even the guild
of adventures wasn't too serious.  I may have lost some potions that
they drank, but really, with all the stuff you can pick up in slash'em
this wasn't that much of a deal.  I did find an athame though (3
actually), which I used a couple of times (mainly to deal with
Demogorgon), and kept in my main inventory.  Somewhere along the way I
also enchanted Nighthorn to +7 (I felt this was more important than
getting weapons +7), got two Movanic (sp?) Devas, (which I guess are
one step up), and lost one Monadic Deva to a purple worm/lurker.  No
big loss.

The other interesting branch was Ludios.  Frag and Gas grenades oh my.
Valk-Smash!!  I did feel a little cheated though that for all the
bullets they were carrying around, I didn't get shot at once.
Otherwise, why lug a bloody heavy machine-gun around?  The grenades
weren't too bad - since I was fire and poison resistant, but I had a
few frights - when I accidentally armed a whole stack and had to run,
and when my minions got caught in the blast - worried about them
getting untame.

Actually, this sounds like an idea for One-Eyed-Sam.  Arm a large stack
of grenades (I think he's not poison resistant), drop them, and run!


I didn't try to kill Sam.  Instead, I gathered all the extra money I
had after getting the regular divine protection, and used it for credit
in his shop.  Along came three baby dragons, and each in turn picked up
the gold and chased out after me to the hall.  All in all, I had about
$400,000 in credit. But I get ahead of myself.


Anyway.

By the time I reached Medusa, I was AC -27 , both Mojo and Ex were +4,
and if this was a vanilla game, I was more than ready to make the
ascension run.  I wasn't sure how much harder slash'em might be, and
besides, I still had to get through Gehenom.  In any case, around this
point I decided that I was going to do a
Genoless-Polyless-Polyselfless-Wishless.  Didn't realize I could have
had reflectionless until the very end.

I crossed Medusa flying, and used Nighthorn to stone her.  Not before
letting my Devas clear out all the water creatures.  See?  I knew they
were good for something.  The castle was 3 levels below Medusa, and
being able to just fly over the moat was a fun experience.  We got rid
of the guards, and dragons (Only one was full sized).  The others were
baby dragons.  The silver dragon of course didn't leave scales.

One of the store rooms had a Gypsy wandering around, but I decided not
to bother - esp. since I was going wishless.

The Valley was interesting.  You don't fall down near the stairs, which
disoriented me a bit, and some of the undead are much more dangerous
than in Vanilla.  <cough> Ghoul Queens <cough>.  Though Vampire mages
and Star Vampires are not to be sneered at either.  The Moloch priest
was hostile - not sure why.  I also had Nighthorn and a bunch of other
equipment cursed, and had to retreat to get some holy water.  I put the
unihorn in my bag of holding to make sure I didn't accidentally (a)pply
it on the way back up.

One piece of equipment that would have really been useful at this point
was a ring of Teleport Control for my Orb, but I didn't find one until
much later - in the Black Market.  Yeah, I should have tried earlier.
Instead I just had to hope that the orb got me to a sufficiently low
level.  Most of the time it worked.


After the valley, the first level was Jubilex. Whack-Whack.  Actually,
I almost died on this level - to a Giant Shoggoth.  More hitpoints than
Demogorgon, and more damage than a rampaging air elemental, not to
mention the ability to corrode everything in my inventory at once.  I
killed it with 28(178) hitpoints left.  I did have a wand of death (and
an wand of digging, but I'm not sure that would have worked) on me as
well as a potion of full healing on me - so I guess I could have played
that safer.  Anyway, from then on, I decided that Giant Shoggoths
warrant a charge of a wand of death (I already found at least 4 of
those, so this wasn't really a waste.  Slash'em really does leave a
_lot_ of equipment lying about).

The next level was Demogorgon.  I had recently met 'Big D' in vanilla,
and I wasn't fond of the memory.  I had half a mind to go back to my
stash and break out the big guns, but then I looked through my
inventory and decided I had enough to take him on:

Just for the record:

Inventory:  ~180hp, AC -29 +4 Excalibur, +4 Mojo
athame,
4 potions of holy water,
potion of full healing,
2 potions of extra healing,
blessed +7 unihorn,
ring of free action,
ring of +2 increase damage,

Orb of Fate (getaway).
Could safely pray.

I also had an amulet of life saving, but elected not to put it on -
preferring my amulet of flying.

I dug a bit and blocked the upstairs with a bolder.  Demo could run
away, but he wouldn't be able to flee up a level.  I started walking
through the maze toward Demo, but then decided that I had better clear
a shorter path.  Too late.  Demo must have seen me via telepathy, and
he started whacking on me.  Curing the illness didn't work the first
time so I quaffed a potion of holy water.  The next turn I engraved
with the athame, and Demo ran.

It kept on like this a for a while.  I would move a few turns (maybe
dig through a wall with my wand of digging), engrave Elbereth and wait
for Demo to show up.  He would show up, and run as soon as he saw me.
Then I would go forward a bit more.  This happened about four or five
times, though I think he did hit me once more.  Anyway, I finally got
to the stairs, engraved, and started whacking him.  Shortly after, it
was over.



